The package OSQP

  https://github.com/osqp/osqp

is not included as part of GALAHAD, but may be
called optionally from GALAHAD's ubersolver QP package.

GALAHAD relies on an environment variable,
and if OSQP has not been installed, these 
are set by default as

  OSQP = -lgalahad_osqp

This allows GALAHAD to function, but any attempt
to use OSQP will result in an error return code.

If you wish to use OSQP ...

1. Download OSQP via git to a suitable location 

  https://github.com/osqp/osqp-fortran

  and thene move into the OSQP directory using

     cd ./OSQP

   we shall refer to this through the shell variable $OSQP

2. Install the package into a suitable subdirectory
   (here ./build/pc64.lnx.gfo for a gfortran build) using Cmake

    cmake -B build/pc64.lnx.gfo \
     -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
     -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON \
     -DOSQP_USE_FLOAT=OFF \
     -DOSQP_USE_LONG=OFF \
     -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5
    cmake --build build/pc64.lnx.gfo --config Release -j

   This is appropriate for default GALAHAD interactions, but
   OSQP_USE_FLOAT=ON and -DOSQP_USE_LONG=ON change from 
   double- to single-precision reals and 64- rather than 32-bit
   integers
   
   You can use other compilers by, e.g., resetting them prior to
   the first call, e.g. (in the bash shell)

      CC=icx CXX=icpx FC=ifx cmake ....
   
   or by using the flags

     -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
     -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \ 
     -DCMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER=ifx \

   but remember to change the name of the build directory (to, e.g.,
   build/pc64.lnx.ifx) if you want to have two or more separate builds

3. Now call GALAHAD with the envirnment variables

      OSQP_BUILD = $(OSQP)/build/pc64.lnx.gfo
      OSQP = -losqpf -losqp \
        -L$(OSQP_BUILD)/lib -L$(OSQP_BUILD)/osqp_sources/out \
        -Wl,-R$(OSQP_BUILD)/lib:$(OSQP_BUILD)/osqp_sources/out

